      "quiz_68_7_q": "What is sublimation?",
      "quiz_68_8_fb1": "Without tree leaves to transpire and block wind, overall evapotranspiration drops, drying the local climate.",
      "quiz_68_8_fb3": "Deforestation reduces the water pump effect, decreasing atmospheric humidity and cloud formation.",
      "quiz_68_8_fb4": "Trees are vital hydrological regulators. Deforestation leads to severe soil erosion and immediate flooding.",
      "quiz_68_8_opt1": "Increases evaporation",
      "quiz_68_8_opt2": "Reduces transpiration and increases runoff",
      "quiz_68_8_opt3": "Creates more clouds",
      "quiz_68_8_opt4": "Has no effect",
      "quiz_68_8_q": "How does deforestation affect the water cycle?",
      "quiz_912_1_fb1": "This rate is too low. The average environmental lapse rate in the troposphere is 6.5°C per kilometer.",
      "quiz_912_1_fb3": "This is the dry adiabatic lapse rate (9.8°C/km) for dry rising air, not the environmental profile.",
      "quiz_912_1_fb4": "This cooling rate is too high. The average atmospheric cooling is around 6.5°C per 1000m.",
      "quiz_912_1_opt1": "~2°C per 1000m",
      "quiz_912_1_opt2": "~6.5°C per 1000m",
      "quiz_912_1_opt3": "~10°C per 1000m",
      "quiz_912_1_opt4": "~15°C per 1000m",
      "quiz_912_1_q": "At what rate does air temperature decrease with altitude (environmental lapse rate)?",
      "quiz_912_2_fb2": "Boyle's Law relates gas pressure to volume, not fluid flow through soils.",
      "quiz_912_2_fb3": "Ohm's Law relates voltage and current, though it shares mathematical forms with Darcy's Law.",
      "quiz_912_2_fb4": "Bernoulli's equation applies to open pipe flow, not flow within saturated media.",
      "quiz_912_2_opt1": "Darcy's Law",
      "quiz_912_2_opt2": "Boyle's Law",
      "quiz_912_2_opt3": "Ohm's Law",
      "quiz_912_2_opt4": "Bernoulli's Principle",
      "quiz_912_2_q": "What law governs groundwater flow through saturated porous media?",
      "quiz_912_3_fb1": "This value is too low. Water requires about 2.45 megajoules per kilogram to vaporize.",
      "quiz_912_3_fb3": "This is the specific heat capacity of liquid water, not its latent heat of vaporization.",
