      "humped_low_clearance_crossing": "Humped / low-clearance crossing",
      "hybrid_city_highway_mpg_because_regene": "• Hybrid city > highway MPG because regenerative braking recovers energy in stop-and-go. Gas cars do the opposite.",
      "hydraulic_fluid_transmits_pedal_force_": "Hydraulic fluid transmits pedal force to calipers. Incompressible = instant response. Absorbs moisture over time.",
      "hydraulic_or_electric_assist_that_redu": "Hydraulic or electric assist that reduces steering effort. Without it, turning at low speed requires enormous force.",
      "hydroplane": "Hydroplane",
      "hydroplane_loss_of_control": "Hydroplane Loss of Control",
      "hydroplane_speed_threshold": "Hydroplane speed threshold",
      "hypermiling_content": "Hypermiling is the art of squeezing every mile from a gallon. Proven techniques: (1) Gentle accel — imagine an egg between your foot and the pedal. (2) Anticipate — read the road 15 seconds ahead so you brake less. (3) Coast to stops — fuel cut-off activates when you lift off the gas, meaning coasting in gear uses ZERO fuel on modern cars. (4) Pulse and glide — gently accelerate, then coast. Works best under 45 mph. (5) Highway cruise at 55-60 mph, not 75 (drag is brutal). (6) Tires at door-sticker PSI. (7) Remove the roof box when not using it. (8) Don't idle — modern engines restart on <0.5 s of fuel, so 30+ sec of idling wastes more than restarting. Combined, these techniques can boost real-world MPG by 20-40% over aggressive driving. Free money.",
      "hypermiling_lab_2": "Hypermiling Lab",
      "hypermiling_lab_3": "Hypermiling Lab",
      "hypothesis_free_text_why_does_kinetic_": "Hypothesis (free text): Why does kinetic energy grow with v² but only linearly with mass?",
      "i_295_merge_65_mph_3_second_following_": "I-295 merge. 65 mph, 3-second following, smooth acceleration.",
      "i_can_beat_that_train": "\"I can beat that train\"",
      "i_only_had_two_beers_i_m_totally_fine_": "\"I only had two beers, I'm totally fine to drive. You're overreacting.\"",
